Fungi are eukaryotic organisms and, as such, are commonly land living, ecological generalists. They are customers of organic compounds and are found in every habitat except for marine and the extremely dry enviroments. In most cases, fungi make optimal use of organic chemicals and transform them using assimilatory power gained through respiration. Respiration, cells, and organic compounds diffuse into the mycelium of the fungi, after which it is broken down and rearranged into useful chemical compounds such as: glycogen, guanine, uric acid, propionic, succinic, and citric acid. These chemical compounds make up the nutritional needs for fungi. However, fungi have never been known to be able to perform photosynthesis. Conclusively, (A) is the correct answer. There are three distinct ways in which fungi reproduce. The first two are asexual in nature, which means they do not involve the fusion of two genetically different cells. The first method produces spores of relatively equal genetic makeup (gemmules). The second way is also an asexual reproductive manner consisting in a single parent breaking into twos. It is called budding. The third way is a sexual reproduction process. Fungi bring a sexual pairwise fusion of cells, namely the formation of zygote and meiosis. There are three major types of reproduction: two asexual and one sexual. Asexual reproduction. Somatic cells in fungi go through cell division without sexual reproduction. Doing so requires divisions to multiply through extension, which is called fission. Fission can be done in a couple of ways: budding and binary fission. Binary fission results in the creation of two genetically identical cells. Budding results in the creation of another genetically identical cell. Sexual reproduction. Sexual reproduction in fungi typically produces reproductive cells known as gametes. Gametes are produced through meiosis. The order of events in sexual reproduction in fungi differs little from other eukaryotes, and the only major difference is that a first cell wall has to be formed after each of the meiotic divisions (mitosis: two nuclei within one massive nucleus. Meiosis results in two nuclei separated by a non-permeable membrane). In most cases in nature, sexual reproduction in fungi occurs via the process to help solve a problem called plasmogamy. This process occurs when the protoplasm of two different species of fungi fuse together and each provide a different nucleus for a sexually produced zygote. The zygote eventually sweats out the non-fusing parent protoplasm. The zygote is temporarily surrounded and protected by a wall. A thick wall develops to contain only the new fungal fusion cell. The new cells are a genetic mix of the two parent cells. This process is essential to prevent the catastrophe of hybrid unviability. These new genetically varied cells are known as the dikaryotic state. A dikaryotic hypha will become dikaryotic mycelium, with hyphae branched to produce new mycelium. During extension, new nuclei are made by a process called karyogamy, when the fused protoplasms divide. New haploid nuclei are made and each undergo paired exchanges to produce four nuclei. This produces a diploid zygote. This zygote forms a new mycelium. Deuteromycota. The majority of fungi divide only asexually, reproducing by the release of haploid asexual spores into the air. They are asexual by definition. Sometimes mistakes occur in classification and they reproduce asexually under the microscope. These can also be referred to as imperfect fungi. The most common modes of sporulation are conidial and cleistothecial. Conidiogen production can occur on any hyphal portion. Some fungi may even produce spores underground. Asexual spores tend to move around actively when dispersed. A sexual cycle exists in some asexual fungi. But sex does not occur in the natural environment. Instead, the spore + spore fusion that occurs after germination at the laboratory (or artificial) gives us genetic diversity in nature without needing too many criteria to determine whether we are dealing with a sexual or asexual organism. Asexually-reproducing fungi are also referred to as the imperfect fungi. One example of a asexual fungus in the imperfectly Fungi. A personal favorite is Penicillium, which was an important discovery because it alone gave rise to the secondary metabolites known as antibiotics. Some fungi produce asexual spores called conidia. These spores are simple, commonly haploid asexually produced building blocks. They are stored as conidiophore chains in a pocket or sac called a phialide. The phialide extends elongated out from the parent hypha in one or more chains. The person in the phialides grows into a dry, sausage looking, tube called a conidium. Conidia are used as the asexual propagation method for most species in the genus Penicillium, other genera, e g, Aspergillus, Cladosporium, and Cephalosporium. These genera are known together as the Penicillium family. Penicillium also produces a sexual fruiting body called a perithecium. Penicillium fruiting bodies are small, usually yellow to greenish-orange, carrot-shaped, and enclosed in a pore called an ostiole. The perithecium grows from a stalk called a stipe, and produces dark purple or blue-black fruiting bodies. Sexual nuclei are formed after the germination of the ascospores. However, in Penicillium, asexual spores are produced before the sexual spores. Making sexual spores inefficient in nature. Penicillium conidia are like normal spores that germinate, grow, and coil to form conidiophores. Abaxial conidiophores are formed first. They look like a slightly curled up cone or pinwheel when they twist away from the parent hypha. Kenar conidiophores grow next. A conidiophore is a special cell that produces conidia. It looks like a candle on a stalk. Coiled conidiophores grow from the binaphthial. The different-coloured conidiophore is there for identification purposes. Coiled conidiophores are called phial